        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "s1:p1",
            "text": "Viola canina, commonly known as heath dog-violet and heath violet, is a species of the flowering plant in the violet family Violaceae. It is native to Europe, where it is found in heaths, fens, and moist woodlands, especially on acidic soils."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p2",
            "text": "It is a herbaceous perennial plant growing to {{Convert|5-15|cm|abbr=on}} tall. The flowers are pale blue, produced from April to July. Colonies of plants may be extensive."
          },
          {
            "id": "s1:p3",
            "text": "It is host to the pathogenic fungi Puccinia violae and Ramularia lactea."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
